From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 02:40:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(web): hold the reader's row while a streaming turn
 re-keys or re-measures rows

Scrolling up a little to re-read a reply that is still streaming worked until
the model's first tool call: the view then jumped to the end of the reply
(-1927px on a 1280x800 desktop, -7164px on an iPhone 14 viewport).

The virtualized transcript keeps native scroll anchoring off and left in-list
height changes to the virtualizer's own per-row scroll adjustment. A streaming
assistant bubble is keyed `responseId:0` until its first item gets a
store-assigned id, then remounts under the new key at the 280px estimate; the
library reads that row's first measurement (a row starting above the viewport
top) as an estimated block above the fold and scrolls by its height minus the
estimate although nothing on screen moved. The same heuristic skips rows that
span the viewport top and defers on iOS WebKit.

Generalize the history-prepend hold into the one in-list hold: on every
virtualizer change (once per measurement batch, before paint) and after every
commit, compare the mounted rows with their list-relative offsets from the
previous hold and move scrollTop by how far the row that was first fully
visible shifted, a renamed row by its bottom edge as before. The library's own
adjustment is off for good so nothing is counted twice; list-relative offsets
keep the list-offset compensation disjoint; a re-keyed row with the same
content holds nothing. Follow-tail at the bottom, the prepend lock release and
synchronous page measurement, and the native-find mode are unchanged.

Not upstream yet: proposed from fix/transcript-hold-position; drop this copy once it merges.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
(cherry picked from commit 28e59914599d2c8794610b690a82c587f58b955e)
Docs: none (fix: the transcript keeps the reader's place while a reply streams; no setting)
